WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:13.300
Music and mood. Music has charms to soothe the savage breast. Even centuries ago, people

2
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:20.420
knew music could change their moods. Generally, loud music makes us feel powerful, soft melodies

3
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:27.100
are calming, and happy beats make our feet move and our faces smile. Now, we're figuring

4
00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:32.800
out why. Scientists have found that music affects how the brain produces the chemical

5
00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:40.520
dopamine. Dopamine is associated with pleasure. When we listen to music we like, our brains

6
00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:47.120
make more dopamine. The chemical makes us feel good. Even just thinking about listening

7
00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:54.040
to a song we love can get the dopamine flowing. In a strange way, this suggests that music

8
00:00:54.040 --> 00:01:01.920
can be slightly addictive. Researchers at McGill University studied how music affects us. First,

9
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.880
they measured how people's bodies responded to music. They found that when people listen

10
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:13.240
to music they like, their heart, pulse, and breathing rates change. Sometimes they feel

11
00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:20.880
chills. These changes also happen when people feel strong emotions. Since dopamine is connected

12
00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.920
with emotions, the McGill team guessed that music would cause the production of dopamine.

13
00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:31.760
To test their theory, they asked eight music lovers to bring in some of their favorite

14
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:37.680
songs. After the participants had listened to the music for a while, the team injected

15
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:43.240
them with a substance that would go to the parts of the brain that collect dopamine.

16
00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:49.480
If those parts were already full of dopamine, the substance would have nowhere to go. Sure

17
00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:56.000
enough, the participants' brains were already flooded with dopamine. To be sure, they tested

18
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:03.120
them again the next day, but with music participants didn't like. That time, the substance did

19
00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:11.200
flow to the dopamine collectors because participants' brains hadn't made dopamine. Even early humans

20
00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:16.240
knew how to play good music for a pick-me-up. Now we know why.
